#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""tuxwell-gui: the Tuxwell control panel (GTK4 + libadwaita) and tray icon.

What this program is
    A thin front end. The real work is done by other pieces, and this window only changes settings and shows state:
      - bin/tuxwell            the background service. It reads ~/.config/tuxwell/tuxwell.conf, so most controls here
                               just write that file and restart the service.
      - bin/tuxwell-hrtf       switches the head-response set (downloads it if needed).
      - bin/tuxwell-test-sound plays the test sound used to compare head responses.
      - audezeforlinux         optional third-party tool used to READ the headset's battery, sidetone and chat mix.
      - pactl / amixer         used directly for the immediate effects (volume, output switching, mic profile).

Layout of this file
    1. constants and small pure helpers (settings, catalogs, audio state, output-switch plan, HID preset write)
    2. Win       the main window: four tabs (Listen, Surround, Headset, More)
    3. Tray      a minimal StatusNotifierItem tray icon (the protocol Discord, qBittorrent, etc. use)
    4. App       the application object (single instance; closing the window hides it to the tray)

Threading rule
    The GTK main loop must never wait on a subprocess (the window would freeze). Anything that runs pactl, amixer,
    systemctl or another program goes through bg(), which runs it in a worker thread and delivers the result back to
    the main loop. Only the handful of tiny /proc and file reads happen directly.
"""
import fcntl, glob, os, re, shlex, subprocess, threading
import gi
gi.require_version("Gtk", "4.0")
gi.require_version("Adw", "1")
from gi.repository import Adw, Gio, GLib, Gtk

